Deep Evidence-Based Investigation Report: Android SDK Setup in GitHub Actions
1. Exact Root Cause & Investigation Findings
A. Why did android-actions/setup-android@v3 output "Wrong version in preinstalled sdkmanager"?
GitHub’s official ubuntu-latest runner image (ubuntu-24.04) comes with a modern Android SDK and Command-Line Tools pre-installed at /usr/local/lib/android/sdk/cmdline-tools/latest (specifically revision Pkg.Revision=20.0 or 22.0).
When android-actions/setup-android@v3 ran, it checked the version of this pre-installed tool against its hardcoded older expectation (v3 expected Command-Line Tools version 16.0).
Because the versions did not match, it logged the message: "Wrong version in preinstalled sdkmanager".
Critical Mechanism: That specific line in the action source code is a non-fatal warning indicating that the action intends to download a separate zip file from Google to replace the pre-installed command-line tools. However, when the action subsequently attempted to download/unzip into the pre-existing system path or invoke outdated arguments, it exited with a fatal error code.
B. Why did upgrading to @v4 with cmdline-tools-version: '15859902' still fail?
Build 15859902 corresponds to Command-Line Tools version 22.0.
The GitHub runner image had Pkg.Revision=20.0 mounted.
Overriding or extracting a secondary Command-Line Tools archive into the runner's protected /usr/local/lib/android/sdk directory encountered path permission conflicts and duplicate cmdline-tools directory structures (cmdline-tools/latest vs cmdline-tools/22.0), causing the action runner script to fail.
C. Is the setup-android action actually necessary?
NO.
Evidence from GitHub's official runner specification (Ubuntu2404-Readme.md):
Environment variable ANDROID_HOME=/usr/local/lib/android/sdk is globally configured on every runner.
Platforms pre-installed: android-34, android-35, and android-36.
Android Build Tools pre-installed: 34.0.0, 35.0.0, 36.0.0.
Android Command-Line Tools and Platform Tools: fully pre-installed and available on $PATH.
Gradle automatically reads $ANDROID_HOME without requiring any third-party setup action. The third-party action was fighting the runner's pre-installed SDK.
2. Project & Gradle Configuration Verification
Root Gradle File (android/build.gradle): Android Gradle Plugin (AGP) 8.13.0 (fully compatible with Java 17 and Android SDK 36).
Gradle Wrapper (android/gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties): Gradle 8.14.3 (gradle-8.14.3-all.zip).
SDK Variables (android/variables.gradle):
compileSdkVersion = 36 (pre-installed on ubuntu-latest)
targetSdkVersion = 36
minSdkVersion = 24
Application Build (android/app/build.gradle): Namespace and Application ID com.expenseflow.app, Version Code 1, Version Name "1.0".
3. Exact Fix Implemented
The cleanest, most reliable solution is to eliminate the third-party setup-android step completely.
Removed android-actions/setup-android.
Kept actions/setup-java@v4 with Temurin JDK 17.
Gradle (./gradlew bundleRelease) runs natively and locates the Android SDK via the runner's built-in $ANDROID_HOME.
Kept all other steps: Bun setup, frozen-lockfile dependency installation, test suite execution (93 tests), Vite web build, Capacitor Android sync, signing logic via GitHub Secrets, and artifact upload.
